1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,547
Training videos leak.

2
00:00:02,547 --> 00:00:09,328
A customer's email, a phone number, an API key left on screen.

3
00:00:09,328 --> 00:00:19,172
Capstan is the screen recorder and editor that hides them before the video leaves your PC.

4
00:00:19,172 --> 00:00:25,516
Record a tab, cut it, blur it, caption it, export the MP4.

5
00:00:25,516 --> 00:00:28,031
No installer, no cloud.

6
00:00:28,031 --> 00:00:30,297
Here is how Capstan fits together.

7
00:00:30,297 --> 00:00:36,499
A PowerShell launcher serves the editor on localhost, and the editor runs in your browser window.

8
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:44,068
Inside, four stages: record, edit, redact and caption, export.

9
00:00:44,068 --> 00:00:50,268
One compositor draws both the preview and the export, so what you see is what you get.

10
00:00:50,968 --> 00:00:53,618
Fleet connections are plain files.

11
00:00:53,618 --> 00:00:55,960
SnapShackle captures come in.

12
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,712
MP4, captions and chapters go out for Scrimshaw.

13
00:00:58,712 --> 00:01:00,708
The Fleet CLI drives it.

14
00:01:00,708 --> 00:01:05,355
There is no model, so no Fairlead or Lazarette.

15
00:01:05,355 --> 00:01:08,222
Speech to text is Windows' own offline recogniser.

16
00:01:08,922 --> 00:01:13,668
Planned: background removal, and a Capstan CLI launcher.

17
00:01:13,668 --> 00:01:18,016
And the one rule that matters: media never leaves this PC.

18
00:01:18,016 --> 00:01:20,291
Nothing is uploaded, ever.

19
00:01:20,541 --> 00:01:22,239
Capstan records one browser tab.

20
00:01:22,239 --> 00:01:28,630
Choose Record screen, then A browser tab, and switch the microphone and computer sound off.

21
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:30,845
Start recording.

22
00:01:30,845 --> 00:01:35,509
Capstan captures only the tab we choose; screens and other windows are refused.

23
00:01:35,509 --> 00:01:41,181
This is a synthetic support console, with a made-up customer, working a ticket.

24
00:01:41,881 --> 00:01:42,788
Stop and save.

25
00:01:43,488 --> 00:01:44,883
The clip is on the timeline.

26
00:01:45,633 --> 00:01:47,076
Play it back.

27
00:01:47,076 --> 00:01:51,634
This is the real recording, with a customer's details and an API key in plain view.

28
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:54,640
Split off the dead start and ripple delete it.

29
00:01:55,181 --> 00:01:57,066
Then add a blur region at the playhead.

30
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:00,320
Place it over the email, the phone number and the API key.

31
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:04,284
It covers every track beneath it, for the whole clip.

32
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:08,989
Scrub forward, and the blur holds as the agent resolves the ticket and opens the next one.

33
00:02:09,689 --> 00:02:12,296
Import the training captions from an SRT file.

34
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:14,866
They are drawn on the video at export.

35
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:15,666
Export.

36
00:02:15,311 --> 00:02:16,111
H.

37
00:02:15,438 --> 00:02:19,003
264 MP4, rendered on this PC, frame by frame.

38
00:02:19,003 --> 00:02:24,067
A software encoder takes over if the hardware one stalls.

39
00:02:24,067 --> 00:02:28,775
Under four seconds here, for fifteen seconds of video.

40
00:02:29,475 --> 00:02:32,617
Now check the file, not the screen.

41
00:02:32,617 --> 00:02:33,654
ffprobe reports H.

42
00:02:33,654 --> 00:02:37,487
264, 1920 by 1080, about fifteen seconds, and no audio track.

43
00:02:37,487 --> 00:02:40,653
The microphone was off, so there is none.

44
00:02:41,353 --> 00:02:43,794
Same frame, before and after.

45
00:02:43,794 --> 00:02:48,238
The email, the phone and the key are gone from the pixels.

46
00:02:48,238 --> 00:02:51,022
The blur is baked into the export.

47
00:02:51,022 --> 00:02:53,946
It is not an overlay anyone can lift.

48
00:02:54,646 --> 00:03:02,425
The saved project hands its captions to the next tool through the Fleet CLI: four captions, ready for Scrimshaw.

49
00:03:02,425 --> 00:03:06,398
Plain files, nothing pushed, nothing uploaded.

50
00:03:07,211 --> 00:03:11,492
To be candid: the console and its customers are synthetic.

51
00:03:11,492 --> 00:03:17,288
Capstan has no model, so no Fairlead, and no approval queue.

52
00:03:17,288 --> 00:03:23,627
It is a single user local tool, and its trust is in what it refuses and what stays on this PC.

53
00:03:23,627 --> 00:03:27,537
Close the window and the host stops itself.

54
00:03:27,537 --> 00:03:33,960
Capstan records locally, hides what must not show, and exports a file you can check.

55
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:35,719
Nothing leaves this PC.

56
00:03:35,719 --> 00:03:39,083
Compute and creativity are the new frontier.
